  ('eafe1861-9067-47b3-ad45-10d58f0da59e','USA Archery affiliation puts New Mexico Chile Peppers in Albuquerque on the same competitive pathway as clubs across the country, running Junior Olympic Archery Development for young shooters alongside an adult archery program and 3D archery for members who want realistic target work. That spread, kids'' pathway, grown-up beginners, and 3D, gives the club a broader footprint than a single-focus group, useful in a city where families often want to shoot together rather than split between separate outfits. Albuquerque''s desert setting and strong regional bowhunting culture make the 3D piece a natural fit alongside the JOAD ladder. Contact the club directly for current practice schedules and how to join.'),
  ('b689de23-50bb-43b1-9252-40670a7437b6','Tucked into the mountains of West Jefferson, New River Archery Club runs as an ASA-affiliated volunteer group focused on 3D archery, the format built around lifelike animal targets that mirrors real bowhunting shots. Northwestern North Carolina''s terrain, all ridges and hardwood cover, makes it a natural home for a 3D-minded club rather than a flat-range target operation. Greg Howell serves as the point of contact, typical for volunteer-run clubs where a member handles inquiries rather than a front desk. Beyond the ASA tie and Greg''s contact info, no further programs or amenities are listed, so anyone interested in shooting rounds or joining should reach out to him directly for the current schedule.'),
  ('e512c405-6cee-4007-8701-02ac6ce27aa1','Just up the road from West Jefferson in Mount Airy, New River Bowhunters is another ASA-affiliated 3D club serving North Carolina''s northwestern corner, though it''s a separate group with its own membership and its own contact, David Smith. ASA affiliation means the club''s shoots follow the scored 3D format popular with bowhunters looking to practice on realistic animal targets before season, rather than straight target-paper archery. Mount Airy''s rural, wooded surroundings suit that kind of course well. No additional detail on lanes, facilities, or fees is available beyond the ASA tie, so the best path for a newcomer is contacting David Smith directly to ask about shoot dates and how to get involved.'),
  ('7d42d4de-d43b-4b7d-b163-c11d53d762be','Next Arrow Archery Academy in Sallisaw, Oklahoma carries USA Archery affiliation and backs it with an unusually full program list: Junior Olympic Archery Development, an adult program, 3D archery, and field archery all under one roof. Running four formats at once, Olympic-style target work, grown-up instruction, 3D animal targets, and field courses across varied terrain, suggests an academy built to keep archers of every age and interest engaged rather than specializing narrowly. Eastern Oklahoma has a strong bowhunting culture, so the 3D and field components likely draw a different crowd than the JOAD side does. Reach out to the academy directly to find out which programs are currently running and how to enroll.'),
  ('a55a9c74-a92c-4860-94f9-19ecf4fac91b','Ninnescah Bowhunters Association takes its name from the Ninnescah River running through south-central Kansas, and the club itself is an ASA-affiliated 3D group based in the small town of Viola. ASA''s scored, animal-target format is built for archers sharpening their form ahead of deer season, and a club naming itself after bowhunters rather than target archers signals where its priorities sit. Henry Boss is listed as the contact, the usual setup for a volunteer club without a staffed office. Kansas''s plains and river-bottom terrain give 3D courses like this plenty of natural cover to work with. For shoot dates, course conditions, or membership questions, reaching out to Henry directly is the way in.'),
  ('a3988d11-9bca-4a4a-bc12-023aa89ca009','A short drive from Viola in Pratt, Kansas, Ninnescah Valley Archers is a separate ASA-affiliated 3D club sharing the same river-valley namesake but its own membership and contact, Aaron Austin. Like other ASA clubs in the region, the format centers on scored shots at lifelike animal targets rather than paper bullseyes, which lines up well with the strong deer-hunting tradition across south-central Kansas. Beyond the ASA tie and Aaron''s contact listing, there isn''t published detail on course length, membership fees, or shoot frequency. Given how many small ASA clubs dot this part of the state, calling or messaging Aaron directly is the most reliable way to confirm current details before making the trip out to Pratt.'),
